'''Indigenous peoples of Balisca''' ({{lang-bl|pueblos indígenas de Balisca}}), '''Indigenous Baliscans''' ({{lang-bl|nativos baliscanos}}) or '''Anhangá-Quetrupillán Natives''' ({{lang-bl|pueblos originarios de Anhangá}}-''Quetrupillán'', {{Literal translation|Original peoples of [[Baliscan archipelago|Anhangá-Quetrupillán]]}}) are those who are part of communities that trace their roots back to populations and communities that existed in what is now [[Balisca]] before the arrival of Illypnians.

In the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], based on {{W|self-identification}} or self-ascription, almost 11 million Baliscan declared themselves to be [[w:Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous]] or first-generation descendants of Indigenous people, that is, 6.90% of the population. The most populous indigenous groups are the [[Kurupiranga]] (including [[Honú-Abá]], [[Murí people|Murí]] (''costeños''), [[Aruanãyará]], [[Roáka'á]], [[Kalútupí]] and [[Kuarupíba]]), [[w:Mapuche people|Wallche]], [[w:Wichí people|Wejwus]], [[w:Caiçaras|Caiçaras]], and the [[Nyengatú people|Nyengatú]].

The number of indigenous Baliscan is defined through the second article of the [[Constitution of Balisca|Baliscan Constitution]]. Additionally, the Baliscan census does not {{W|Race (human categorization)|categorize individuals by race}}, using the {{W|culture|cultural}}-{{W|ethnic group|ethnicity}} of indigenous communities that preserve their indigenous languages, traditions, beliefs, and cultures. As a result, the count of indigenous peoples in Balisca does not include those of mixed heritage who have not preserved their indigenous cultural practices. Genetic studies have found that most Baliscan are of partial indigenous heritage.

Efforts have been made, centering on the [[Treaty of Yvyra'ara]], and intensifying with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] to increase the standing of Indigenous people in wider Balisca society and achieve {{W|social justice}}, and facilitate {{W|Indigenous decolonization}}. Traditional Indigenous cultures have enjoyed significant revivals across the archipelago, which was further bolstered by the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. A number of socio-economic initiatives have been instigated with the aim of "closing the gaps" and aiding the restoration of indigenous sovereignty. [[W:Reparations (transitional justice)|Political and economic redress]] for historical grievances is also ongoing (see [[Indigenous nations in Balisca]]).

The bulk of contemporary Indigenous peoples in Balisca mostly reside both in their traditional mountain villages as well as increasingly in Balisca's urban areas. There are also the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|plains indigenous peoples]], which have always lived in the lowland areas of the [[Marañón basin]]. Ever since the independence of Balisca, armed resistance (such as the [[Tinguiririca conflict]]) and other efforts have been under way in indigenous communities to protect and revive traditional cultural practices and preserve their distinct traditional languages. In addition, several indigenous communities have become extensively involved in the {{W|homesteading}}, {{W|sustainable agriculture}} and {{W|ecotourism}} industries with the goal of achieving increased {{W|economic self-reliance}} and maintaining {{W|cultural integration}}.
